clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:4:- A judged question concerns one figure for a named period or one event. Write a question, not an assertion. A figure or event must be able to determine the outcome on the due date. For a figure, the question names the period it covers or the day it is measured at, and the material that settles it.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:5:- A topic's wording may ask about more than one thing. Judge the one the topic's outcome names as what is tracked. List every other part of the topic's wording that the question leaves out, a second figure or event, a condition or a comparison, in not_judged, each with one sentence on why it is not the one tracked. Never fold two things into one ladder: "Will the regulator approve the drug for a second use, and how many patients will take it in the first year?" is judged on the approval when that is what its outcome names, and the patient count is listed; "How much will the carrier's fuel bill be next year, and will its pilots strike?" is judged on the fuel bill when that is what its outcome names, and the strike is listed.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:6:- Return no_ladder with the reason, and ladder null, in seven cases: the topic has no main figure or event, naming none, or naming several with none of them the main one; nothing in the topic can be checked on a date, which for an event means that neither the topic nor the item gives a date and the topic names no period, unless it is a pending regulatory decision, for which use the company's current fiscal year-end; the figure is a company's figure for a quarter or a half-year, which is evidence to a yearly question; the figure covers a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve months, and neither the topic nor the item gives the day it is published; the figure is for a year that has already ended on the item's day; the figure is for a year beyond next year, unless the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation; or the question belongs to another company or to a matter that no theme in the request's themes covers, in which case name that company or matter in the reason. Do not rewrite a figure for a year beyond next year as an event to keep it; a numeric completion target may define the state of an event that is itself the topic.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:9:- A question about the company's revenue, profit, share, product progress or its own obligations is about the company: object is its ticker, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it. A question about what happens in the world, such as licensing, interest rates or aggregate spending, rather than the resulting effect on one company, belongs to a theme: object is a theme id from the request's themes. A question whose tracked outcome is a government's or regulator's decision to permit, restrict or ban a class of products or trade belongs to the theme that covers it, also when the product is the company's own: whether a health ministry lifts its import ban on one drug maker's vaccine belongs to the theme that covers that country's drug approvals. When the tracked outcome is the company's own sales, shipments or revenue under such a decision, the question is about the company, as is a proceeding against the company itself, such as an investigation of its conduct or a fine. A question about another company's own figure, or about a matter no listed theme covers, has no object here: return no_ladder.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:12:- Define concrete, verifiable conditions now. Do not write a ladder whose thresholds depend on an unspecified future target. Conditions must not overlap or leave gaps: exactly one must hold when resolved; avoid vague "partial" outcomes.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:13:- An event is answered in event, in at most three outcomes. happened is the condition under which the event the question asks about has happened by the due day. ruled_out says who can rule the event as a whole out before the due day, and how: by_whom names the company, authority, court or party whose decision it would be; decision is one full sentence that names them and says what they decide against the event as a whole, or that they formally abandon or withdraw it, after the item's day. The due day arriving without the event is no one's decision and is never written there: it is what is left. ruled_out is null only when no one's decision can rule the event out before the due day. Write no other condition: what is left, neither of the two, needs none, and where both would hold the event counts as happened. A difference inside an outcome, such as an approval with conditions against one without, is not an outcome of its own; when the topic's wording names it, list it in not_judged. A regulator reviewing a merger gives: happened, the regulator has cleared the merger by the due day; ruled_out, by_whom the regulator or the parties to the merger, decision the regulator blocks the merger or the parties withdraw the filing. An authority's investigation or examination that the question asks to end in a published decision or conclusion always has a ruling out: happened, the authority publishes that decision or conclusion by the due day, whatever it finds; ruled_out, by_whom the authority, decision the authority formally abandons or withdraws the proceeding without it. The proceeding still going on the due day is neither, and a decision to go on with it is not a ruling out. A safety agency examining a carmaker's defect gives: happened, the agency publishes its findings; ruled_out, by_whom the agency, decision the agency formally closes or withdraws the examination without publishing any.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:16:- reference is what this figure or event is measured against when the topic opens, quoted in the item's own words. Its kind is expected when the item states what the market expects: a consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline. For a figure only, when the item states no expectation and gives the amount most recently reported for the same quantity (the same thing measured the same way: not a part of it, a ceiling on it or another quantity), that amount is the reference and its kind is last_reported; a reported amount is never called an expectation. Otherwise reference is null. value is the number the item states, with value_to its upper end when the item states a range; when the item gives it in words alone, value is null: never supply a number the item does not state. The ranges of a figure state the outcome against a reference that has a value: the middle range holds that value, or runs from at or below a stated range's lower end to at or above its upper end, and the ranges on either side are outcomes above and below it. When reference is null or has no value, the ranges are your own estimate of the outcomes that could happen.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:17:- favourable_end, for a figure about a company: higher when a higher outcome is good for its business, lower when a lower one is, with one sentence why. A result and its direction are separate: capital spending can build capacity or hurt returns. For an amount the company owes, guarantees or has committed to pay, when neither the topic nor the item settles which end is good for the business, because they argue both ends or say nothing on it, it is lower; when the topic's own words say that more of it is good, it is higher. A retailer's lease guarantees that the item says win store sites and also weigh on its credit are lower; an airline's aircraft purchase commitments that the topic tracks as the fleet growth its plan needs are higher. For a theme's figure it is n/a.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:22:- For an event, name the date at which its outcome is assessed: stated when the topic or the item gives it; period_end when it is the last day of the period the topic names, or the company's current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision that has no date.

codex
**The required field is a reasonable idea to examine at $0, but it is not ready to build as proposed.** It can make the model enumerate parts; it cannot guarantee that the enumeration is complete or that the part marked “judged” is actually answered.

1. **Is it the right lever?** It addresses the largest observed class: **6 of 78 medium sends** omitted a part, sometimes alongside another fault. But the proposed field targets L, while Q—the next-largest class—includes fused decisions. It has not yet shown that either class will improve. Also, **13 of 78** includes one code refusal; **12 of 78** had agreed answer faults. [Effort result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/effort/RESULT.md), [trace review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/effort/review.md)

   The prior A2 probe is a warning: it removed omissions on its motivating topics but also listed outcomes already decided and sources used to settle the question. A required list could repeat that problem. [A2 result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/RESULT-A2.md)

2. **The proposed checks are identity checks only in a narrow sense.** Code can check that a quoted span exists and that two identifiers or exact strings match. It cannot establish that the model found **every** relevant part, that a span expresses a complete part, or that “judged” describes what the answer actually decides. If the model omits a part from both lists, the checks pass.

   The proposed contract also narrows the existing rule: the form covers **all the topic’s wording**, including the description, not only its outcome. Exact copied words can reject a valid paraphrase or a combined part that preserves the meaning. Requiring exactly one `judged` part could also force a false split of one event or mishandle a justified refusal. Those are behavioural decisions, not mere identity checks. [Reading form](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md), [D397 and D436](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS.md:741)

3. **Before preparation, do one $0 counterexample audit of the contract.** Read the stored L and Q failures alongside correct answers and justified refusals. For each, write a valid proposed field and `not_judged` list, then check whether the proposed contract accepts it. Include parts stated only in the description, paraphrases, combined parts, an event’s already-decided outcomes and a case where the model leaves the same part out of both lists.

   **Move to design** only if a general contract accepts the sound answers, exposes the omissions it claims to address, and does not force the model to split or invent parts. **Close this proposed contract** if it either rejects sound answers or passes the same omitted-part failure. A later schema or prompt change still needs the full blind prompt audit, stored-data checks and a separately decided probe.

The Claude reference is a useful lead, but one writer’s result does not isolate model family or prove that a provider switch would fix the step.

**CONSENSUS: no, because the field’s coverage and identity contract must first survive the $0 counterexample audit; do not build or send the proposed arm yet.**
